    "q-343429": {
      "dom_id": "step-q343429",
      "name": "Question 9",
      "problem": "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-1, & x\\in [-2,0] \\\\ 2, & x\\in (0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $4$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{1}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{\\pi (2n-1)}sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.At $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=1,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(1)={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite our Fourier series as\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that at $x=1,$ we have\n\n$$\nsin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi }{2})=sin\\,(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi )=(-1)^{n+1}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is $\\text{continuous}$ at $x=1,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(1)=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{(2n-1)\\pi \\cdot 1}{2}) \\\\ 2 & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2n-1}\\cdot (-1)^{n+1} \\\\ \\frac{3}{2} & =\\frac{6}{\\pi }{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} \\\\ {\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "select",
          "dom_id": "selectList-343429-0",
          "tag": "custom-select",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "0",
              "dom_id": null,
